":"question","note":"Eduqas publishes these papers from the first exams (June 2017) to June 2025, with the autumn 2020 and 2021 sittings. Each paper asks about every set text, so the topics here are the texts: pick the ones you studied. Component 1 Section B and Component 2 Section B are set on pairs of texts, so each pair is a topic. Component 3's unseen prose is from one of two periods, and its poems are unseen. Component 4, the prose study, is coursework and has no paper.","count":400,"firstYear":2017,"lastYear":2025,"sample":[{"id":"a720u10-jun25-01-i","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"1(i)","m":20,"t":["chaucer"],"w":1193,"h":83,"x":"Re-read lines 976–999 from “‘I have,’ quod she, ‘a soule for to kepe…’” to “…and up he wente.” Examine Chaucer’s presentation of May in these lines.","st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-01-ii","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"1(ii)","m":40,"t":["chaucer"],"w":1193,"h":118,"x":"“As Chaucer presents them, we can neither admire nor sympathise with any of the characters.” With close reference to relevant contexts, examine this view of The","st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-02-i","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"2(i)","m":20,"t":["donne"],"w":1193,"h":83,"x":"Re-read ‘Holy Sonnet VI’ on pages 179–180. Examine Donne’s use of imagery in this poem.","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-02-ii","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"2(ii)","m":40,"t":["donne"],"w":1193,"h":91,"x":"Donne’s poetry has been described as “intensely physical”. With close reference to relevant contexts, explore this view of Donne’s poetry.","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-03-i","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"3(i)","m":20,"t":["milton"],"w":1193,"h":83,"x":"Re-read lines 758–779 from “In plain then…” to “…both body and mind?’” Examine Milton’s presentation of Eve in these lines.","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-03-ii","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"3(ii)","m":40,"t":["milton"],"w":1193,"h":118,"x":"“As Milton presents it, the desire for more is inspired by the devil.” In the light of this comment, and with close reference to relevant contexts, examine Milt","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-04-i","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"4(i)","m":20,"t":["blake"],"w":1193,"h":82,"x":"Re-read ‘The Divine Image’ on page 90. Examine Blake’s use of poetic techniques in this poem.","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-04-ii","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"4(ii)","m":40,"t":["blake"],"w":1193,"h":117,"x":"How far do you agree with the view that “while the imagery and subject matter are of their time, the poems still speak powerfully to us”? In the course of your ","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-05-i","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"5(i)","m":20,"t":["rossetti"],"w":1193,"h":82,"x":"Re-read ‘De Profundis’ on page 153. Examine the ways in which Rossetti presents intense feelings in this poem.","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-05-ii","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"5(ii)","m":40,"t":["rossetti"],"w":1193,"h":118,"x":"How far would you agree with the view that “Rossetti’s poetry has little appeal to a non-religious reader”? In the course of your writing, you must make close r","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-06-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"6","m":60,"t":["hardy-eliot"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"“We are fascinated by their ideas but rarely comforted by their poetry.” In the light of this view, and with close reference to relevant contexts, examine conne","st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-07-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"7","m":60,"t":["hardy-eliot"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"Both poets have been accused of “…a kind of self-obsession which stands in the way of compassion for their fellow man”. In the light of this view, and with clos","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-08-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"8","m":60,"t":["yeats-abse"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"“They are first and foremost story-tellers.” In the light of this comment, what connections have you found between the ways in which Yeats’ and Abse’s poetry ap","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-09-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"9","m":60,"t":["yeats-abse"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"“In both collections of poetry we witness exuberant celebrations of love in its many different forms.” In the light of this comment, and with close reference to","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-10-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"10","m":60,"t":["hughes-plath"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"“Death is presented with a sense of fascination rather than fear or dread.” In the light of this comment, and with close reference to relevant contexts, examine","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-11-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"11","m":60,"t":["hughes-plath"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"“In both poets’ writing, landscapes are presented as having special power and significance.” In the light of this comment, and with close reference to relevant ","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-12-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"12","m":60,"t":["larkin-duffy"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"“Effective poems often have the power to capture a range of contradictory emotions.” In the light of this comment, and with close reference to relevant contexts","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-13-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"13","m":60,"t":["larkin-duffy"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"“In the seemingly minor details of domestic life, both poets discover profound truths.” In the light of this comment, and with reference to relevant contexts, e","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-14-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"14","m":60,"t":["heaney-sheers"],"w":1193,"h":144,"x":"“Both poets demonstrate an intense awareness of the physical world.” In the light of this view, and with reference to relevant contexts, explore connections bet","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1},{"id":"a720u10-jun25-15-q","p":"a720u10","s":"JUN25","l":"15","m":60,"t":["heaney-sheers"],"w":1193,"h":170,"x":"“The poets are emotionally engaged but the poems are somehow completely unsentimental.” In drawing connections between the ways in which Heaney and Sheers prese","or":1,"st":1,"ms":1,"er":1}]}